  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "Many languages use a verb (to fear) where English says be afraid; look for the natural verb rather than an adjective. The polite phrase I'm afraid carries no fear at all and needs a formula for regret.",
    "grammar": "Afraid is followed by of plus a noun or an -ing form, by to plus a verb, or by a that-clause: afraid of dogs, afraid of falling, afraid to ask, afraid that it will rain. It needs be, and never comes before a noun.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "The stress is on the second syllable; the first vowel is the weak ə, as in about."
  },
